<h2 class="content-box pad-box-mini" style="background-color: #941728; color: #ffffff;">Build your first complete language generator</h2>
<p>An <strong>N-gram model</strong> predicts what comes next by looking at the previous few items. The letter <em>N</em> tells you how many items are considered together. In a character bigram model, N is 2: the model uses one character as the context and predicts the next character.</p>
<p>For example, if the character <strong>b</strong> is followed by <strong>a</strong> three times and by <strong>y</strong> once in the training text, the model estimates a 3/4 chance of <strong>a</strong> and a 1/4 chance of <strong>y</strong> after <strong>b</strong>. Generating text means repeatedly sampling from these learned probabilities.</p>
<p>This demonstration connects the earlier lessons: counts become conditional probabilities, sampling chooses the next character, and negative log-likelihood measures how well the model predicts a sequence.</p>
<p><strong>What you will do:</strong> train a small character-level model, generate text from it, change its settings, and measure how well it predicts held-out text.</p>
<p><strong>Video focus (under 10 minutes):</strong> convert counts into conditional probabilities, generate text one character at a time, and evaluate held-out text with negative log-likelihood and perplexity.</p>
<h2 class="content-box pad-box-mini" style="background-color: #941728; color: #ffffff;">From counts to conditional probabilities</h2>
<p>For a context <em>c</em> and possible next character <em>x</em>, Equation (5.1) estimates the conditional probability from counts. Read <span aria-label="p hat">p̂</span> as &ldquo;p hat&rdquo;; the hat says this probability is estimated from the training text. The numerator counts how often <em>x</em> followed <em>c</em>. The denominator counts every occasion on which <em>c</em> had a following character. Dividing makes the estimated probabilities for all possible next characters add to 1.</p>
<div style="display: flex; align-items: center; margin: 0.75rem 0;">
<div style="flex: 1 1 auto; min-width: 0; text-align: center; overflow-x: auto;"><math style="font-size: 1.2rem;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block" aria-label="Equation 5.1. The estimated probability p hat of next character x given context c equals the count of c followed by x divided by the total count of c."><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>|</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mtext>count</mtext><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mtext>count</mtext><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math></div>
<span style="width: 3.5rem; flex: 0 0 3.5rem; text-align: right;" aria-hidden="true">(5.1)</span></div>
<p>A bigram character model uses one preceding character as context. A larger N-gram uses a longer context. Longer contexts can capture more structure, but many contexts may occur rarely or never. One implementation detail matters when you run the course code: <code>ORDER</code> is the <strong>context length</strong>, not the conventional value of <em>N</em>. Therefore, <code>ORDER = 1</code> produces a bigram model (<em>N</em> = 2), <code>ORDER = 2</code> produces a trigram model (<em>N</em> = 3), and so on.</p>
<h2 class="content-box pad-box-mini" style="background-color: #941728; color: #ffffff;">Generation</h2>
<ol>
<li>Begin with an initial context.</li>
<li>Look up the counts of characters that followed that context in the training text.</li>
<li>Normalize the counts into probabilities.</li>
<li>Sample the next character.</li>
<li>Append it, update the context, and repeat.</li>
</ol>
<p>This is a genuine generative model. It is small and count-based rather than neural, but it defines and samples from a conditional probability distribution.</p>
<h2 class="content-box pad-box-mini" style="background-color: #941728; color: #ffffff;">Evaluation and smoothing</h2>
<p>The negative log-likelihood of held-out text adds the surprise assigned to each actual next character. In Equation (5.2), <em>T</em> is the number of characters being predicted, <em>t</em> identifies one position, <em>c</em><sub><em>t</em></sub> is the context at that position, and <em>x</em><sub><em>t</em></sub> is the character that actually followed it. The &sum; symbol&mdash;<strong>sigma</strong> (&ldquo;SIG-ma&rdquo;)&mdash;adds the losses, and dividing by <em>T</em> gives the average. <strong>exp</strong> means the exponential function; it reverses the natural logarithm used in the loss. Lower negative log-likelihood and lower perplexity both mean that the model assigned greater probability to the held-out sequence.</p>
<div style="display: flex; align-items: center; margin: 0.75rem 0;">
<div style="flex: 1 1 auto; min-width: 0; text-align: center; overflow-x: auto;"><math style="font-size: 1.2rem;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block" aria-label="Equation 5.2. The average negative log-likelihood equals negative one over T times the sum from t equals one to T of the natural log of p of x t given context t. Perplexity equals the exponential of the average negative log-likelihood."><mtable columnalign="right left" rowspacing="0.35em"><mtr><mtd><msub><mtext>NLL</mtext><mtext>avg</mtext></msub></mtd><mtd><mrow><mo>=</mo><mo>&minus;</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>T</mi></mfrac><munderover><mo>&sum;</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover><mi>log</mi><mspace></mspace><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>|</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mtext>perplexity</mtext></mtd><mtd><mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mtext>NLL</mtext><mtext>avg</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></math></div>
<span style="width: 3.5rem; flex: 0 0 3.5rem; text-align: right;" aria-hidden="true">(5.2)</span></div>
<p>An unseen context&ndash;character pair would otherwise receive probability zero. Its logarithm would then be undefined, producing an infinite loss. The demonstration uses <strong>add-one smoothing</strong>: it acts as though every possible next character had been seen once, so every vocabulary character receives a small nonzero probability. This prevents the evaluation from collapsing because of one unseen pair.</p>
